Give scanf a working `%[` and stop `%s` truncating at 31 bytes.

cosmo 4.0.2's __vcscanf (behind scanf, fscanf, sscanf, vfscanf …) has no
scanset. `%[` falls into the specifier switch's default, `items =
einval()`, and returns -1 — which is EOF. A caller looping
`while (fscanf(f, fmt, …) != EOF)` therefore reads nothing and reports
nothing. That is exactly how X's font directories are read (libXfont2
FontFileReadDirectory, `%1023s %1023[^\n]`): the Windows Xvfb accepted
fonts.dir, parsed its count, added zero of its 334 fonts and logged no
error, serving 11 fonts where Linux serves 509. libXfont2's BDF reader
and libfontenc's encodings.dir reader use the same form.

Second, a `%s` (or `%[`) with no width into a caller-supplied buffer was
capped at a hard-coded 32-byte buffer: the 32nd byte onward stayed in the
input for the NEXT directive, so `sscanf("<40-char word> tail", "%s %s")`
returned the word split in two. C11 7.21.6.2 puts no bound there; the
caller owns the buffer size. `%ms` keeps its growable 32-byte start.

The upstream fix is a full rewrite of the scanf engine (jart/cosmopolitan
5ddb5c2ada, after 4.0.2, with a new __vcscanf signature), so it cannot be
lifted alone. This adds the scanset to the 4.0.2 decoder instead, byte
strings only (`%l[` still refuses, as before): `^` inverts, `]` first is
a member, `-` first or last is a member, `a-z` is a range, no leading
whitespace is skipped, and matching zero characters is a matching failure
rather than EOF.

Verified on a Windows 10 VM with a 21-case battery plus a real fonts.dir,
compared against glibc: the stock build diverged on 18 lines, the patched
one is byte-identical to glibc's output.

